  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure will continue to dominate the weather pattern\nacross the Northeast through Saturday before moving offshore on\nSunday. Partly to mostly sunny skies are expected today through\nthe first half of Sunday, although more in the way of cloud\ncover is possible at times across the mtns and towards the\nCanadian Border. High temperatures will remain well above avg\ninto the 50s to lower 60s through Friday before cooling a few\ndegrees for Saturday and Sunday as the high gradually moves to\nour east. Winds will remain largely offshore out of the W at\n10-20 kts. No precipitation is expected through at least the\nfirst half of Sunday, leading to increasing rainfall deficits. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nGlobal models have been pretty consistent over the last 48 \nhours in developing a strong low pressure system off the \nSoutheast U.S. Coast. What has been less consistent is it's \nnorthward track toward our area, and that is going to have \nimplications on potential impacts to our area. The ECMWF has the\nmajority of members clustered offshore and the GFS is clustered\ncloser to the coast. Both models have been struggling in these \nscenarios recently and the solution has been ending up a hybrid.\nTherefore, confidence is increasing that at least a part of our\narea sees a wetting rain. The coast of course is the most \nlikely candidate right now with probabilities for greater than \nan inch of rainfall sitting around 50 percent. This decreases \nheading inland pretty quickly for two reasons: The main axis of \nmoisture has been pretty consistently modeled to remain \noffshore, and inland rain is going to depend on the low phasing \nwith a trough moving in from the west. This system has also been\nconsistently progressive, so at this point confidence is \nincreasing in a beneficial soaking rain with no indications of \nflood impacts.\n\nConfidence is low on wind and coastal flooding impacts as that \nis highly track dependent, but it is worth noting that the \nwindow for this system looks to be Sunday night into Monday, \nright at the start of a period of high astronomical tides. The \nMonday afternoon astrotide is forecast to be 10.8 feet at \nPortland which means this will have to be monitored closely as \nonly a little over a foot of surge would have us pushing minor \nflood thresholds. Again, this system looks progressive so this \nmay be the only tide we have to worry about and thank goodness \nas we will be pushing an 11.5 foot astrotide by the middle of \nnext week.\n\nFollowing the system into midweek will be a push of cold air \nthat brings daytime temperatures below normal as high pressure \nbuilds back into the region with mean troughing aloft.",
